  "## The question that started this

  『murakumo cli を install したら、その pc が推論 provider として murakumo に
  登録されて、推論に参加し、報酬を受け取れるようにできているか』

  Measured on 2026-09-09 the answer was no, in four separate places, and the
  fourth one is the interesting one.

  ## What was actually there (measured, not read off the docs)

  - **No CLI.** `cloud-murakumo-cli` did not exist. `join.cljc` declares the
    native tier's `:install :curl-sh`, which is a tier descriptor and not an
    installer; no published install path existed on npm, brew or anywhere else.
  - **Enrollment worked.** `POST /infer/nodes` is public and live: 66 nodes
    enrolled, 56 of them Community.
  - **Admission was decorative.** Every Community node was `:pending`. The only
    writer of `:node/admission` in the entire codebase was enrollment itself,
    and it can only write `:accepted` for an operator-authenticated Secure
    node. **No route existed that could move a Community node to admitted.**
    The Secure/Community ADR's sentence — `registration alone is not capacity`
    — was true in the opposite sense to the one intended.
  - **And the gate was not a gate.** The queue never read `:node/admission`.
    Any `did:key` with a self-signed CACAO could claim `host-large-model` work
    and be credited for whatever it returned; 22 accounts already had. All 612
    `native-relay` runs in the ledger record `:run/proof :verified` with
    nothing having verified any of them — `relay_worker.clj` says so in its own
    header.
  - **Reward existed but did not leave.** `murakumo.infer.credits` settles
    `:run/shares` correctly, and its own docstring forbids adding a fiat payout
    path without revisiting ADR-2607995000 first.

  So the open enrollment endpoint was a form for a queue nobody served, and the
  admission field was a lock with no key and no door.

  ## The two decisions that were the owner's

  **Reward form: credits by default, with a credits→USDC payout rail behind an
  owner approval.** ADR-2607995000's membrane moves in exactly one direction
  for exactly one class of holder: a Community provider may withdraw credits it
  EARNED by serving inference. Nothing else about the membrane changes.

  **Admission: automatic qualification.** A node that answers a
  server-issued challenge correctly and in time is admitted without an operator
  in the loop. The alternative — measure, then wait for a human — leaves the
  Community pool empty for as long as nobody is pushing the button, which is
  the state this ADR exists to end.

  The owner also asked that FLOPS be part of the accounting.

  ## What FLOPs are for, and why not memory×time

  `murakumo.infer.credits` prices the OWNED fleet by memory×time, because what
  is scarce on ten Mac minis is resident memory: a node holding 11 GB of a
  shard cannot host anything else with it. That is the right unit for a shard
  ring the operator placed and the wrong one for a stranger's PC that dials in,
  serves a whole request end to end and leaves — there is no shard, no
  placement, and no memory the operator reserved. What that machine contributed
  is arithmetic, so `local-murakumo.provider-flops` counts arithmetic: two FLOPs
  per active parameter per token, prompt and generated tokens alike, which
  understates a long-context request and is therefore the safe direction for a
  payout. An unknown model measures `nil`, never `0`, and every consumer has to
  keep those apart.

  ## What lands

  | piece | where | what it decides |
  |---|---|---|
  | qualification | `local-murakumo.provider-qualification` | a nonce-derived challenge; correct and in-window ⇒ `:accepted` |
  | the claim gate | `local-murakumo.edge-queue/claim-decision` | native-tier work requires an admitted node |
  | delivered FLOPs | `local-murakumo.provider-flops` | arithmetic recorded beside the price |
  | payout | `local-murakumo.payout` | earned credits → USDC on Base, owner-approved |
  | the CLI | `network-awai/cloud-murakumo-cli` | enroll → qualify → serve, in one command |

  ### The qualification is honest about its own reach

  It proves that, inside one bounded window, an endpoint controlled by this
  `did:key` answered a challenge that did not exist before it was issued. It
  does NOT prove the answers came from local hardware, from the model the node
  named, or from a model at all. Saying so in the namespace docstring is the
  point: the mechanisms that bound that fraud are delivered-FLOPs accounting,
  canary re-execution of sampled jobs (not built), and a human in front of
  every payout. A qualification is an admission gate, not an attestation —
  `local-murakumo.device-p256` is the attestation and is a narrower, different
  thing.

  ### The claim gate is tier-shaped, not blanket

  A blanket gate would have deleted the browser swarm, whose workers are
  unenrolled `did:key:pending-*` placeholders by design. `join/tiers` already
  says a browser can take media-postproc, small shards, prompt eval and
  embarrassingly parallel work and cannot host a large model — so browser-tier
  kinds stay open to unenrolled dids, which is what those tiers ARE, and the
  four native-only kinds require an admitted node. Operator-authenticated
  claims bypass the gate entirely: the owned fleet is trusted by custody, and
  requiring it to qualify would have made this change an outage for ten Mac
  minis.

  ### The payout rail's three separations

  1. **Only earned credits are payable.** `:run/shares` are; grants and topups
     are not. Without this, buy-credits-then-withdraw is a laundering path and
     would be the first thing anyone tried.
  2. **A request debits; a settlement does not.** Credits leave the account
     when the provider asks. Debiting at settlement leaves a window in which
     the same credits are payable twice, exactly as long as a human approval
     takes.
  3. **Nothing in the codebase moves money.** `settle` records a transaction
     hash the owner produced from the treasury Safe. No key is reachable from
     this path, by construction rather than by convention.

  The starting rate is 125 credits per USD against a published buy side of 95,
  a ~24% spread covering the treasury cut, gas and the refund reserve. It is
  derived from the buy price, not measured, and lives in `payout/default-policy`
  as data so it can be tuned without touching the decisions.

  ## The deploy that broke production, and what it changed

  The first deploy of this work 500'd **every route on api.murakumo.cloud**,
  including `/health`, for about four minutes before rollback:

      500 {\"error\":\"Error: No item 2 in vector of length 1\"}

  Cause: `worker.cljs` was written as five nested promise chains inline in the
  route table, and one of them was a single closing paren short. The parens
  still balanced across the file, so the `cond` simply closed early — every
  branch after it became an unconditional expression, and `(seg 2)` ran for
  every request. It compiled, bundled, packaged, digest-verified and deployed.

  Nothing between the edit and production had an opinion, and the reason is
  structural rather than careless: `local-murakumo.routes` is the pure,
  portable half and has 372 tests; `local-murakumo.worker` is the half that
  deploys and had **none**, because it needs a Worker runtime the nbb suite
  does not have. The suite was green throughout.

  Two things landed because of it:

  - `scripts/verify-worker-bundle.cljs` loads `dist/worker.js` and probes six
    paths whose answers need no storage, failing on any 500. Confirmed to fail
    on the broken bundle (5 of 6 FAIL) before it was confirmed to pass on the
    fixed one — the gate was seen red first.
  - the handlers were rewritten as named functions, so the route table branches
    are one line each. The mistake is still possible; it is no longer invisible.

  ## Evidence

  - `cloud-murakumo-api` tests 333 → 372, assertions 1911 → 2069, `npm test` green.
  - Both new gates mutation-tested: admitting every node fails 9 tests; making
    bought credits withdrawable fails 4.
  - `routes_test` asserts the refusal AND the admission for the same job, and
    the double-claim test uses a second admitted node so it cannot pass at 403
    without ever reaching the 409 path.
  - The CLI's CACAO round-trips through `cacao.edge.verify` — the same verifier
    the Worker runs — and the round-trip test caught a real first-run bug:
    `generate!` returned no private key, so the very first `murakumo join` on a
    fresh machine would have crashed, and every subsequent run would have
    worked.
  - `nbb.edn` had two dead classpath entries (`kotoba-kir`, renamed to `osaho`;
    `kotoba.lang.text`, never added when `clojure.string` was retired). The
    repo's whole nbb suite could not run at all until they were fixed.

  ## What is NOT done

  - **Output verification.** Nothing re-executes a sampled job on a Secure node
    and compares. `:run/proof` now says `:self-reported` where that is the
    truth, which is a prerequisite for building it, not a substitute.
  - **The CLI repo is private.** A stranger cannot install it until the owner
    makes `network-awai/cloud-murakumo-cli` public; publishing a repo is
    outside the standing authorization and is deliberately left as the owner's
    action.
  - **No npm registry publish.** `npm i -g github:network-awai/...` works
    without a registry token; a real `@murakumo/cli` name needs one.
  - **The payout rail has never settled.** The routes, the accounting and the
    refusals are tested; no USDC has moved, because moving it is the owner's
    action and no code here can take it.
  - **Community pool depth is 1.** This machine. A pool of one is a working
    mechanism and not yet a market."}]
